I go to check this file /home/sysadmin/backups/ where I find a read me file and a infrastructure.kdbx file 
A KeePass database (.kdbx) holding “infrastructure credentials”
There was no keyfile alongside it, so it was master-password protected only.

Cracking the KeePass master password
I exfiltrated the database to my attack box (served it over HTTP from the target, pulled it with wget),
then extracted and cracked the hash:

keepass2john infrastructure.kdbx > kdbx.hash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt kdbx.hash
john --show kdbx.hash

Once I had the master password it was time to loot the vault 

